WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.200
My guest this week

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.670
is the Pulitzer Prize winning

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:06.060
American photographer, Lynsey Addario.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.810
Ukraine, Afghanistan, South
Sudan to name but a few,

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.060
she has been there with her camera

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.510
at the majority of the
conflicts in the 21st century.

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.840
I want to hear about the
stories behind those images

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.300
and what it's like living, working,

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.873
existing on the front lines of these wars.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.950
Lynsey, thank you so much for joining me

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:42.060
and joining all of our viewers.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.020
I know it's rare for you

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.330
to be sitting in a seat safely,

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:48.630
away from what you are covering
normally with your camera.

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:50.880
You've come to the world's
attention many times

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.500
with your work over the years.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.990
But very recently, at the
beginning of this year,

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:58.590
in fact, just a few weeks after
the war started in Ukraine,

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:01.380
you did come to the
attention of many people

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:04.710
because you took a photo
of a Ukrainian family

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:08.190
of four trying to cross a
bridge who had been killed.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:09.390
And that photo ended up

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:11.700
on the front pages of the New York Times.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.250
Can you tell me a bit about
how you came to that seat?

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:17.040
So, I went and I went directly to the east

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.070
and was covering the
east for about 10 days

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:21.720
before the war started.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.610
When the war began, I went to Kiev.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.620
And immediately, was covering sort of,

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:29.010
daily missile strikes and the destruction

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:31.890
and those who had been killed and injured.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:36.750
And so one day I was
watching images come out

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:40.320
from the wires and others
who went to the Irpin Bridge.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.220
And there were, you know,
hundreds of civilians crossing,

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:47.820
the elderly women children
crossing out of Irpin

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.410
in all of those suburbs.

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:53.310
And so, we made our way toward the bridge

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:54.693
through side roads.

00:01:56.446 --> 00:01:59.250
And as we approached there were injured,

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:02.130
the territorial defense
soldiers were taking injured

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:05.220
over to sort of a brick
wall off to the side.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:06.960
So, we ran across the street.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.560
And almost as soon as
we got behind that wall

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:11.820
a round came in.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.490
So, it was either a mortar
round or a tank shell.

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:15.510
We're not sure.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:18.660
It came in and hit sort
of off to the distance.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.630
And then, the Ukrainian
military fired back.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:24.720
And so, my security advisor
said, "Would you like to go?"

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.030
And I said, "No, because everyone knows

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:29.130
this is a civilian evacuation route.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.410
And those rounds are probably going

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:34.800
toward a Ukrainian base
that's off in the distance."

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.810
But how wrong I was.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.940
The next round came in closer.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:42.000
And so, we dove for cover and
as soon as we pop back up,

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.290
the next round came in right
next to us essentially.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:49.200
And it was sort of equidistant between us

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.980
and a mother and her two
children and a church volunteer

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:55.023
that was helping usher them to safety.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.390
It was very chaotic.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:00.720
And obviously, we were
all sort of in shock

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.180
because the round had
landed very close to us.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.953
Everything was very dusty.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:07.170
And our security advisor

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:11.340
immediately went to the
aid of, of the injured

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.280
and to a soldier who was nearby.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:18.030
And by the time he
called us over, you know,

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.460
the protocol is, we wait
to be told, you know.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:21.690
It's clear. You can come.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:24.660
And so, by the time he called us over,

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:27.480
I ran across the street
not knowing what to expect.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.130
Obviously, I knew there
was someone injured,

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:31.770
but I anticipated it being a soldier.

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:35.850
And although there were
civilians crossing steadily

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:37.680
and even during the strike, I mean,

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:40.560
because nothing was shielded.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.840
So people had to continue to move.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:44.580
And so when I got there,

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:48.780
I came upon these four lifeless bodies.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:51.000
Of course, I didn't know if they were dead

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.383
or alive at that point.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:57.810
I noticed sort of the tiny
little moon boots of a child.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:01.230
And as a mother, that was sort of,

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:04.350
incredibly jarring and devastating moment.

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:09.103
And I was trying to survey but
I was also kind of in shock.

00:04:09.103 --> 00:04:11.580
One side of my brain was sort of saying,

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:13.800
you have to start photographing

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:15.480
and you have to take these pictures

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:18.930
because I knew that I had
just witnessed a, you know,

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:22.350
something that was an intentional
targeting of civilians.

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:25.080
And so, I took a few photographs.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:26.690
But rounds were still coming in.

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:30.847
Our security advisor,
Steve was saying, you know,

00:04:30.847 --> 00:04:33.780
"Hurry up, work very quickly
and we have to leave."

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:36.900
And so, I kind of moved around the scene

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:40.920
and taking a few photographs,
thinking in my head also,

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:42.510
I have to be respectful.

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:45.270
And, you know, perhaps I
shouldn't show the faces.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:47.340
So I was trying to take pictures

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:50.850
that showed the faces and
that didn't show the faces

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:53.190
so that we had some options.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:55.110
Well, the image that I'm talking about,

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:57.660
or certainly the version I've seen,

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:00.150
you don't necessarily look at
the faces actually the way,

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:02.948
now you've said that, the way
that you look at the image.

00:05:02.948 --> 00:05:04.172
You can.

00:05:04.172 --> 00:05:05.820
You can see them.
You can see them.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:08.010
But it's more you look
at the scene of a family.

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:08.843
Exactly.

00:05:08.843 --> 00:05:09.793
Exactly.

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:14.280
I've been covering war for over 20 years,

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:17.160
and I know that there are
so many images I've taken

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:21.210
that have never been published
because they're graphic.

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:24.990
And so I was thinking of
that as I was shooting,

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:26.820
as horrible as that sounds

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:28.650
because obviously I've just witnessed

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:30.030
the most devastating scene.

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:32.910
But I'm also a photojournalist
and that's my job.

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:36.540
And so, I was trying different angles.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:39.360
And frankly, I didn't think the picture

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:42.408
could ever be published
because it was civilians.

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:45.300
Do you know the impacts of that photo?

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:46.133
Yeah.

00:05:46.133 --> 00:05:48.430
I mean, it's been used by I think,

00:05:49.707 --> 00:05:53.250
the United States Ambassador
to the United Nations

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:57.480
to talk about the intentional
targeting of civilians.

00:05:57.480 --> 00:05:59.580
It's been used repeatedly as proof

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:01.950
that civilians were targeted because,

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:04.020
I was there, I witnessed the run up.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:07.627
And I know that whoever
was firing those rounds

00:06:07.627 --> 00:06:10.620
was bracketing onto that position.

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:11.820
You mentioned being a mother.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:14.403
Has that changed the job for you?

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:16.193
Yeah.

00:06:16.193 --> 00:06:17.820
I mean, it's definitely changed the job

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:20.700
in the sense that I'm more cautious now

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:23.700
than I was before I had children.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:26.220
I think to a sort of normal person

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:27.540
who doesn't do this for a living,

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:28.980
they probably can't see that.

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:32.850
But for me, making decisions
on a minute to minute basis

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:36.240
in a war zone, I know
I'm being more cautious.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.430
I know I'm not going sort of,

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:41.910
all the way to the front or as far forward

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:43.860
as some of my colleagues might be going.

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:46.650
You also are now seeing,

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:49.560
perhaps when you see an image
where you create an image,

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:51.570
when you see the reality of a child

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:53.730
who is dead in front of you

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:56.070
would've always affected you, I imagine.

00:06:56.070 --> 00:06:57.750
Always.
Has it changed though?

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.250
Yes, it has changed.

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:02.400
I think, you know, I photographed,

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:07.140
I have photographed children
dying in Iraq, in Afghanistan,

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:11.790
in Somalia during the Horn
of Africa drought in 2011.

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:13.740
And it was always devastating.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.720
But I think now as a mother,

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:18.390
the first thing I do is sort of put myself

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:20.160
in the shoes of that mother.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.850
And I know, you know,
I live in constant fear

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:26.670
that something will happen to my children.

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:28.680
So you're trying to keep yourself safe,

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.320
but you are also trying to
document at the same time.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:32.160
Of course.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:33.150
And perhaps processing it

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:34.680
in a slightly different way.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:39.360
I mean, would you say you have
become desensitized though?

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:40.193
No.

00:07:40.193 --> 00:07:41.730
Or do you have to be in any way?

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:42.990
No. Not at all.

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:47.460
I mean, I think I have ways of dealing

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:49.980
with my emotion in that moment.

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:52.950
But, you know, part of that
is just really focusing

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:56.040
on the task at hand, which
is making photographs

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:57.930
and looking through the view finder.

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:00.903
That definitely helps me
focus on taking pictures.

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:03.853
But I'm not desensitized at all.

00:08:03.853 --> 00:08:06.840
I mean, I cry pretty much all the time

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:08.640
when I'm photographing.

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:11.310
I get very, very
emotional. And that's okay.

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:14.820
I mean, I don't have an issue with that.

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:17.253
I don't know what my
colleagues think of me,

00:08:17.253 --> 00:08:19.830
but, you know, I think it's normal.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:21.510
I mean, I'm a human being.

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:23.760
I mean, you talked about hanging back

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:25.290
because of the risk as well,

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:26.580
not necessarily going
straight to the front.

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:29.640
And yet, when I asked you
about that particular image,

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:32.010
it sounded like it's very instinctive

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:34.230
when you know you're
not where you need to be

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:36.510
and you are heading towards danger.

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:37.343
Correct.

00:08:37.343 --> 00:08:39.480
And that's obviously what I struggle with

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:42.540
because if I'm close and something happens

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:45.120
a little closer or a
little further from me,

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.540
but closer to the
frontline, I'll likely go.

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:52.410
But I think, where I'm
positioning myself is sort of,

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:53.338
a little removed back

00:08:53.338 --> 00:08:57.120
and then making judgment
calls every day, you know.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:59.760
If I hear that a school has been hit

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:02.910
and 30 children have been
killed, I will go, you know.

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:06.480
If I hear that a building has
been hit on the frontline,

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:09.060
but no civilians were
killed, I'm likely not to go.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:10.830
So, I'm weighing sort of,

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:13.590
what will I gain from risking my life.

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:14.940
Although that building's been hit,

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:16.260
but no civilians have been killed.

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:19.200
I mean, this talks to the
idea of, not you per se,

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:23.820
but a criticism of those in
your field of being war junkies,

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:27.630
trying to find the very worst to document.

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:30.090
And being in places they don't need to be,

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:32.758
sometimes causing perhaps more risk.

00:09:32.758 --> 00:09:34.470
What do you say to that?

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:36.630
You know, for me, the highest toll of war

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:38.190
is civilian casualties.

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:40.110
So that's not being a war junkie,

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:41.580
that is documenting reality.

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:43.763
And the reality is, we are in a situation

00:09:43.763 --> 00:09:46.380
where one country has invaded another.

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:51.380
And every civilian who dies is
on account of that invasion.

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:55.260
And so I think that if I'm in a position

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:58.950
and civilians are being killed,
I have to document that.

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:02.460
That is a very important part of the job

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:03.870
of someone who covers conflict.

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:06.450
And presumably, for you
in this work that you do,

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:07.740
you don't obviously just cover war.

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:10.740
You covered humanitarian
disasters, climate change,

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:12.330
the effects on the planet on that

00:10:12.330 --> 00:10:14.670
but when it is about civilians,

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:16.140
it sounds like the justification

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:17.910
you have to make to
yourself, to your family,

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:19.920
and to those who could be critical,

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:22.560
is that you are providing evidence.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:23.393
Yeah.

00:10:23.393 --> 00:10:24.810
And that evidence has been used already

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:26.130
by the United Nations.

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:27.630
Have you taken pictures

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:31.563
of people killing others of
the actual killers before?

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:35.550
I mean, I've taken pictures of people

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:37.770
who have been accused of killing others,

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:41.040
but I have not been there
sort of, in the moment.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:42.928
I just wonder what that's
like as well, you know,

00:10:42.928 --> 00:10:46.350
seeing people do the very worst
they can do to each other.

00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:47.183
I think people

00:10:47.183 --> 00:10:48.810
are very smart about
journalists, you know.

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:51.300
They obviously don't
allow us access to things

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:53.640
when they're killing civilians, you know.

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:57.930
I think that there was a
time in the Korengal Valley,

00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:02.010
I was with the American
troops, the 173rd Airborne.

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:03.810
I had spent two months living

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:06.483
on the side of a mountain with the 173rd.

00:11:07.530 --> 00:11:09.330
Me and a colleague, Elizabeth Rubin,

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:11.250
for the New York Times Magazine.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:15.210
And the point was to
understand the nuances of war

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:18.600
and why so many civilians
were dying in Afghanistan.

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:22.230
And in fact, Lieutenant Colonel Olson,

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:25.830
who was the commander of the
Korengal Valley, was very smart

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:29.130
because he had this
philosophy of transparency

00:11:29.130 --> 00:11:31.200
because he believed
that journalists needed

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:34.140
to understand sort of the nuances of war

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:36.690
to understand why civilians
were being killed.

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:41.430
And so, we were in Rock Avalanche,

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:45.030
which was in the movie,
the documentary "Restrepo"

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:46.320
that, I don't know if you've seen it,

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:48.150
but it's extraordinary.

00:11:48.150 --> 00:11:51.060
And so, we were in that operation

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:52.860
where we jumped out of helicopters

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:53.693
in the middle of the night

00:11:53.693 --> 00:11:55.410
into the heart of Taliban territory,

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:59.250
literally walked with everything
we owned for six days.

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:01.770
And eventually we were
ambushed by the Taliban.

00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:03.960
And we were ambushed from three sides.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:05.850
And there was a huge gun battle.

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:08.970
Three soldiers were shot, one was killed.

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:12.870
And in the retaliation
of the American troops,

00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:14.970
I was standing with some of the soldiers.

00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:17.430
Everyone was very shaken up, of course.

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:19.207
And one of the soldiers said,

00:12:19.207 --> 00:12:21.690
"Look, they're using Afghan women

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:23.430
and children as human shields."

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:26.730
And he literally had me look
through the scope of his gun.

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:28.560
And there were women and children

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:31.470
on the roofs of the building
where they were shooting from.

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:35.550
And that is a case where it
was incredible to witness.

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:38.100
And I could only have witnessed that

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:40.770
because I had spent so much
time with these troops.

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:43.770
And really, was willing to risk my life

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:45.150
and to be there in the middle

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:47.310
of Operation Rock Avalanche.

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:50.040
A shot you took during that maneuver.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:53.160
We're gonna take a look at that.
Can you take me through it?

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:54.450
There is an image,

00:12:54.450 --> 00:12:56.250
the aftermath of that ambush,

00:12:56.250 --> 00:12:58.290
where it's a very dusty picture.

00:12:58.290 --> 00:13:03.270
It is a scout team carrying
the body of Sergeant Rougle.

00:13:03.270 --> 00:13:06.600
And he's in a body bag.
And it was after that.

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:09.330
And the scout team, Clonard

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:12.270
who is sort of leading the way, is crying.

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:13.470
And they're walking through

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:16.320
this very sort of desolate landscape.

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:19.890
And I remember shooting that
photograph and asking myself,

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:21.480
what are we doing here, you know.

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:24.960
What are we doing here years after 9/11?

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:27.060
Fighting in the middle of the mountains

00:13:27.060 --> 00:13:28.980
where there are no people, you know?

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:30.870
And it just felt like such a waste

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:33.120
that this young man had died for nothing.

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:35.880
And in fact, a few years later,

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:39.510
the American troops pulled
out entirely from that area.

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:40.920
There's another image of yours

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:43.410
I wanted to bring to mind
and for us to have a look at

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:46.080
and talk about an 18 year old woman

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:49.650
in labor, stranded in 2009.

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:51.900
Tell me about that and
how that came about.

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:54.773
So in 2009,

00:13:54.773 --> 00:13:57.990
I won a MacArthur Fellowship
that is in America.

00:13:57.990 --> 00:13:59.370
It's called the Genius Grant.

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:03.090
And it's essentially given
to all different disciplines,

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:04.740
from a cellist to a scientist,

00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:09.600
to a photographer where I was
paid a salary for five years

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:12.360
with no strings attached
to just focus on my work.

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:15.810
And I felt a lot of pressure to decide

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:17.490
what to focus on with my work

00:14:17.490 --> 00:14:19.890
because it was the first
time I could decide

00:14:19.890 --> 00:14:22.530
not be assigned a story.

00:14:22.530 --> 00:14:24.750
And so, I started doing research.

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:27.840
And I decided to focus
on maternal mortality

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:31.230
and why over 500,000 women a year

00:14:31.230 --> 00:14:33.810
were dying in childbirth at that point.

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:36.270
This was 2009, as you said.

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:37.920
And Afghanistan,

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:40.170
where I had been working
for a decade at that point,

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:43.203
had one of the highest rates
of maternal death in the world.

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:47.760
And that was because there
was such a bad road network.

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:51.420
Women who were pregnant or in labor

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:54.810
didn't have access necessarily
to medical professionals

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:58.050
or to clinics, or hospitals
that were staffed with doctors.

00:14:58.050 --> 00:14:58.883
They just couldn't get
where they needed to get?

00:14:58.883 --> 00:15:00.690
They just couldn't get
to where they needed to.

00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:02.790
They had to take a donkey for 12 hours.

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:05.100
And imagine being in labor, you know,

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:06.420
getting on the back of a donkey.

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:09.810
And so, I went to very remote villages

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:11.130
in Badakhshan Province,

00:15:11.130 --> 00:15:13.740
which was one of these
provinces with high numbers.

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:17.550
And on the way back, I
was riding with Dr. Zeba,

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:19.830
who was also helping me translate.

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:23.490
And we noticed these two women
on the side of a mountain.

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:25.230
And there was not a man with them,

00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:27.750
which in Afghanistan is very rare.

00:15:27.750 --> 00:15:31.650
And we stopped the car and
said, "What are you doing here?

00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:32.910
Do you need help?"

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:34.770
And it turned out the woman on the right,

00:15:34.770 --> 00:15:36.750
Noor Nisa was in labor.

00:15:36.750 --> 00:15:41.750
And Noor Nisa's husband's first
wife had died in childbirth.

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:44.400
And he was so determined to not lose her

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:47.580
that he managed to get
a car in the village.

00:15:47.580 --> 00:15:49.800
But when we met them,
their car had broken down,

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:51.930
so they were stranded
on the side of the road.

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:53.400
So I said, "We'll, just get in my car.

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:54.780
I'll take you to the hospital.

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:56.340
We're going toward Fayzabad,

00:15:56.340 --> 00:15:58.440
which was the capital of the state.

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:02.190
And they said, "No, we need
permission from the husband.

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:03.060
So, we'll wait.

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:06.697
And so, I turned to Dr. Zeba and I said,

00:16:06.697 --> 00:16:08.160
"Okay, you have to find the husband.

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:09.660
There's one road in the whole province.

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:12.030
He's obviously on that road," you know?

00:16:12.030 --> 00:16:14.560
So she found him, of
course, pretty quickly

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:16.650
and brought him back.

00:16:16.650 --> 00:16:19.110
And the whole family piled in my car

00:16:19.110 --> 00:16:21.150
and we took them to the hospital.

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:24.420
And everyone asked me
if she delivered safely

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:25.860
and if I took photographs.

00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:27.840
And yes, she delivered safely.

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:29.580
But no, I did not take photographs

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:32.100
because I felt like I changed the course

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:34.707
of that story with my
presence by taking them.

00:16:34.707 --> 00:16:36.330
But you took a photo?

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:37.860
I took about three frames,

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:40.020
and that photograph is
one of those frames.

00:16:40.020 --> 00:16:42.630
And that's when you had
met and just seen it?

00:16:42.630 --> 00:16:44.280
Cause it's so strikingly the blue.

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:46.350
Exactly. I met them.

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:48.300
We asked their story.

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:49.620
And then, we asked permission

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:52.380
right before Zeba left
to find the husband,

00:16:52.380 --> 00:16:54.870
we asked permission for me
to take a few photographs.

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:56.160
Do you think on balance,

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:58.067
being a woman has been a
help to you in this field?

00:16:58.067 --> 00:16:59.425
A huge help.

00:16:59.425 --> 00:17:00.870
Rather than a hindrance?

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:01.703
Yes.

00:17:01.703 --> 00:17:03.150
Everyone thinks it's a hindrance,

00:17:03.150 --> 00:17:07.020
but in fact, it's a huge
help because people -

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:09.930
First of all, I have
access to men and women.

00:17:09.930 --> 00:17:12.060
And particularly when I'm working

00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:14.460
in countries like Afghanistan and Somalia,

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:17.613
where men and women are
usually segregated by gender.

00:17:18.780 --> 00:17:21.990
And people underestimate women
I find, and they still do.

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:24.690
And so they sort of discount me and say,

00:17:24.690 --> 00:17:26.217
Okay, sure, sure. Go do -

00:17:26.217 --> 00:17:27.474
It's nice to be underestimated, isn't it?

00:17:27.474 --> 00:17:28.383
Yeah I love being underestimated.

00:17:28.383 --> 00:17:30.034
Over-deliver.
I love it.

00:17:30.034 --> 00:17:30.867
Yeah.

00:17:30.867 --> 00:17:31.700
It's one of those things.

00:17:31.700 --> 00:17:34.080
But I mean, I suppose
on the other serious,

00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:36.270
much more serious side
of it is that, again,

00:17:36.270 --> 00:17:39.120
when you are thinking and
calculating risks, you know,

00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:41.250
rape as a weapon of war,

00:17:41.250 --> 00:17:44.520
is a unique tool wielded against women.

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:48.540
Yes. And for me, I've
been kidnapped twice.

00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:51.600
The first time was in Iraq,
and that was only a day.

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:54.420
And it turned out, compared to Libya,

00:17:54.420 --> 00:17:56.850
was quite sort of, it was manageable

00:17:56.850 --> 00:18:00.780
in the sense that we were
working outside of Falluja.

00:18:00.780 --> 00:18:04.080
I was working with a colleague
from the New York Times.

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:07.290
We had heard a helicopter of
American troops went down.

00:18:07.290 --> 00:18:08.520
We went to find it.

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:10.950
We got caught by insurgents who thought,

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:13.800
maybe we were with the
American occupation.

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:16.080
We finally convinced
him we were journalists.

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:19.320
They held us until dusk, and
then eventually let us go.

00:18:19.320 --> 00:18:20.670
Obviously, it was terrifying.

00:18:20.670 --> 00:18:24.000
We had guns to our heads
for about 11 hours.

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:26.130
We didn't know if we would survive.

00:18:26.130 --> 00:18:30.720
Libya was much more
violent, much more dramatic.

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:33.030
We were four journalists in one car.

00:18:33.030 --> 00:18:35.280
Originally, we had been two in each car,

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:38.520
but one of the driver's brothers

00:18:38.520 --> 00:18:40.440
had been shot at the front line.

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:42.480
So he quit in the middle of the battle

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.253
and we ended up in one car.

00:18:45.210 --> 00:18:47.970
We had to decide how long to stay.

00:18:47.970 --> 00:18:50.820
That is not an easy discussion,

00:18:50.820 --> 00:18:52.260
because with four journalists,

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:55.170
everyone has a different
perspective on how long to stay,

00:18:55.170 --> 00:18:59.160
what our needs are, how
dangerous the situation is.

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:02.400
By the time we decided to pull
back east toward Benghazi,

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:06.693
we ran into one of Gaddafi's
troop's check checkpoints.

00:19:07.650 --> 00:19:09.930
It was incredibly hostile.

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:11.370
The rebels we had been covering

00:19:11.370 --> 00:19:13.830
started opening fire on that checkpoint.

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:17.250
So we were literally in a wall of bullets.

00:19:17.250 --> 00:19:19.140
The men were pulled out of the car.

00:19:19.140 --> 00:19:21.660
Me as the only woman, I was
sort of left sitting there

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:23.860
because they didn't
know what to do with me.

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:26.310
But the car was not armored.

00:19:26.310 --> 00:19:28.200
So I had to get out of the car

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:30.843
because I knew that I
needed to get behind cover.

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:33.540
I crawled across the backseat.

00:19:33.540 --> 00:19:36.750
And when I jumped out, one
of Gaddafi's troops sort of,

00:19:36.750 --> 00:19:39.480
came to me and was pulling at my cameras.

00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:41.010
Instinctively, I was pulling back,

00:19:41.010 --> 00:19:42.150
even though there were bullets,

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:47.100
literally going zoom zoom, you
know, next to, all around us.

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:48.630
We finally made it to cover

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:51.690
behind the sort of, checkpoint building.

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:55.140
And we're all told to lie
face down in the dirt.

00:19:55.140 --> 00:19:57.480
We each had a kalashnikov
put to our heads,

00:19:57.480 --> 00:20:00.210
and we were about to be executed.

00:20:00.210 --> 00:20:02.103
We were begging for our lives.

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:05.587
The commander came over
at that point and said,

00:20:05.587 --> 00:20:08.070
"You can't execute
them. They're American."

00:20:08.070 --> 00:20:10.680
Which this was all in Arabic.

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:13.683
And Anthony Shadid
later translated for us.

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:16.200
You know, in retrospect,

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:18.570
I have no idea why they
spared us being American,

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:19.650
because in the Middle East,

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:21.950
a lot of people don't
like our foreign policy.

00:20:22.980 --> 00:20:25.833
And so, we were tied up and blindfolded,

00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:29.580
and essentially for the next
three days, we were beaten up,

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:32.610
tied up, blindfolded,
threatened with execution.

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:36.780
And for me, as the only woman,
I was touched repeatedly.

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:37.860
I was not raped.

00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:40.140
But of course, in that time,

00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:42.090
being tied up and blindfolded,

00:20:42.090 --> 00:20:44.910
I thought of the many, many women

00:20:44.910 --> 00:20:47.760
I have interviewed over the
years who have been raped.

00:20:47.760 --> 00:20:52.760
And so I sort of ironically
looked to them for strength

00:20:52.950 --> 00:20:55.680
because I thought, well,
if they survived it,

00:20:55.680 --> 00:20:58.620
and if it happens to me,
I can survive it as well.

00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:01.140
Are are you living just minute to minute?

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:01.973
Minute to minute.

00:21:01.973 --> 00:21:03.300
Yeah. I mean in a kidnapping,

00:21:03.300 --> 00:21:06.060
there is no sort of an hour down the line.

00:21:06.060 --> 00:21:10.920
It is literally every
second is survival mode.

00:21:10.920 --> 00:21:14.160
It is, what can I do to
get through this fear?

00:21:14.160 --> 00:21:16.683
What can I do to get through this pain?

00:21:17.880 --> 00:21:20.730
I mean, it sounds like
the scene from a film,

00:21:20.730 --> 00:21:21.870
and yet it's your life.

00:21:21.870 --> 00:21:24.000
And I believe Jennifer Lawrence

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:26.730
has been interested in
your life or there was

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:28.860
She was.
talk of a film?

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:29.820
She was.

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:31.750
We spent about three years together

00:21:31.750 --> 00:21:35.160
when Warner Brothers optioned
the rights to my memoir.

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:37.687
I wrote a memoir after that called,

00:21:37.687 --> 00:21:41.190
"It's What I Do, A Photographer's
Life of Love and War."

00:21:41.190 --> 00:21:43.980
And that memoir was optioned

00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:46.740
almost immediately by Warner Brothers.

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:49.020
Steven Spielberg was slated to direct,

00:21:49.020 --> 00:21:51.273
and Jennifer Lawrence
was slated to play me.

00:21:52.260 --> 00:21:55.103
And that went on for
three years if not longer.

00:21:55.103 --> 00:21:55.936
What's it like

00:21:55.936 --> 00:21:58.680
taking Jennifer Lawrence to war zones?

00:21:58.680 --> 00:21:59.910
I took her to the border

00:21:59.910 --> 00:22:02.640
with South Sudan when
it was very, very tense.

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:05.760
She wanted to understand
what it feels like

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:08.220
to feel that fear and to be, you know,

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:10.770
to be close to a war zone.

00:22:10.770 --> 00:22:13.590
Obviously, we had to
worry about her safety,

00:22:13.590 --> 00:22:14.640
so I couldn't take her.

00:22:14.640 --> 00:22:15.720
You got an extra headache.

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:17.460
You gotta worry about Hollywood A lister

00:22:17.460 --> 00:22:18.720
while you dealing with everything else.

00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:20.850
It was very stressful let's just say,

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:23.580
when I put her on the plane
to, you know, from Uganda.

00:22:23.580 --> 00:22:24.990
I couldn't walk for a week.

00:22:24.990 --> 00:22:26.193
My back went out.

00:22:27.270 --> 00:22:31.680
But she eventually dropped out.

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:35.550
And so now it is being
made into a limited series.

00:22:35.550 --> 00:22:36.383
Okay.

00:22:36.383 --> 00:22:38.850
So a TV series?
A TV series. Correct.

00:22:38.850 --> 00:22:39.683
Okay.

00:22:39.683 --> 00:22:41.100
But at least Jennifer Lawrence

00:22:41.100 --> 00:22:42.750
now knows what it's like to do your job.

00:22:42.750 --> 00:22:43.583
She does.

00:22:43.583 --> 00:22:44.670
She's just come out with a movie

00:22:44.670 --> 00:22:46.110
playing a character named Lynsey.

00:22:46.110 --> 00:22:46.943
I hope I helped!

00:22:46.943 --> 00:22:49.260
Can't think where she got
that inspiration from.

00:22:49.260 --> 00:22:51.090
But what have you learned then,

00:22:51.090 --> 00:22:52.770
from being in these scenarios,

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:56.370
coming in and coming out
of some of the worst things

00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:57.840
we can do to each other?

00:22:57.840 --> 00:22:58.673
Yeah.

00:22:58.673 --> 00:23:03.183
I mean, I guess I've learned
the capacity for survival,

00:23:04.110 --> 00:23:07.503
for resilience, for forgiveness.

00:23:08.400 --> 00:23:10.500
The fact that human beings

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:15.500
can be equally evil as they
can be amazingly generous

00:23:16.620 --> 00:23:19.950
and yeah, resilient.

00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:21.540
I think that I've learned a lot

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:22.800
from the people I've covered.

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:26.400
But I think we have to look
at the ability of human beings

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:29.520
to live both within those situations

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:31.113
and to survive them after.

00:23:31.950 --> 00:23:33.120
Well, thank you very much

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:35.040
for taking us behind the lens with you.

00:23:35.040 --> 00:23:36.780
Thank you.
Lynsey.

00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:38.940
And thank you so much for being with us.

00:23:38.940 --> 00:23:41.553
Until we meet again,
take care and goodbye.

